# A pipeline whose last stage stops reading early, like `head`, should behave exactly like one
# that reads everything: the script continues and exits 0. This used to fail intermittently (see
# git history for this file for the original bug report) whenever an earlier stage was still
# working when `head` stopped -- the script would silently halt at that statement and exit 1,
# with no error printed anywhere, including inside `try`.
#
# Root cause: a job's own last stage can run in its own thread (e.g. `head`, which can_block), and
# execute::source (src/lang/execute.rs) joined that thread with a bare `?`, unlike Job::eval's own
# internal joins (src/lang/job.rs), which already ignored a SendError there as the normal signal
# that nothing downstream is listening anymore. That's exactly what happens once a pipeline's
# result reaches this script's own output sink (a black hole, since nothing here consumes it): the
# TableInputStream handle gets dropped as soon as it's forwarded, so any row a still-running earlier
# stage (here, `where`, whose condition sleeps to make the race reliable) sends afterward fails with
# a SendError -- benign, not a real failure. Fixed by filtering it the same way at that join too.
#
# The marker below is only printed if the script gets past the pipeline, so a regression shows up
# as a plain output difference (see tests/pipeline_early_exit_halts.crush.output). The exit status
# is checked too, since the test runner expects 0.

seq 0 100 | where {sleep 100ms; $true} | head 1
echo "reached"
